nt-size:11.0pt;font-family:\"Bookman Old Style\",serif'>&nbsp;<\/span><\/p>\r\n  <\/td>\r\n  <td width=114 valign=top style='width:85.65pt;padding:0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt'>\r\n  <p class=MsoNormal align=center style='text-align:center'><span lang=EN-US\r\n  style='font-size:11.0pt;font-family:\"Bookman Old Style\",serif'>&nbsp;<\/span><\/p>\r\n  <\/td>\r\n  <td width=237 valign=top style='width:177.85pt;padding:0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt'>\r\n  <p class=MsoNormal><span lang=EN-US style='font-size:11.0pt;font-family:\"Bookman Old Style\",serif'>Adult\r\n  female : 4.4 <\/span><\/p>\r\n  <\/td>\r\n <\/tr>\r\n<\/table>\r\n\r\n<p class=MsoNormal><span lang=EN-US style='font-size:11.0pt;font-family:\"Bookman Old Style\",serif'>&nbsp;<\/span><\/p>\r\n\r\n<p class=MsoNormal><span lang=EN-US style='font-size:11.0pt;font-family:\"Bookman Old Style\",serif'>&nbsp;<\/span><\/p>\r\n\r\n<p class=MsoNormal><span lang=EN-US style='font-size:11.0pt;font-family:\"Bookman Old Style\",serif'>&nbsp;<\/span><\/p>\r\n\r\n<p class=MsoNormal style='text-autospace:none'><span lang=EN-US\r\nstyle='font-size:9.0pt;font-family:\"Arial\",sans-serif'>Homocysteine is an amino\r\nacid in the blood.Epidemiological studies have shown that elevatedhomocysteine<\/span><\/p>\r\n\r\n<p class=MsoNormal style='text-autospace:none'><span lang=EN-US\r\nstyle='font-size:9.0pt;font-family:\"Arial\",sans-serif'>in the blood is related\r\nto a higher risk of coronary heart disease, stroke and peripheral vascular\r\ndisease.<\/span><\/p>\r\n\r\n<p class=MsoNormal style='text-autospace:none'><span lang=EN-US\r\nstyle='font-size:9.0pt;font-family:\"Arial\",sans-serif'>Other evidence suggests\r\nthat homocysteine may have an effect on atherosclerosis by damaging the inner<\/span><\/p>\r\n\r\n<p class=MsoNormal style='text-autospace:none'><span lang=EN-US\r\nstyle='font-size:9.0pt;font-family:\"Arial\",sans-serif'>lining of arteries and\r\npromoting blood clots.However,a direct causal link hasn't been established.<\/span><\/p>\r\n\r\n<p class=MsoNormal style='text-autospace:none'><span lang=EN-US\r\nstyle='font-size:9.0pt;font-family:\"Arial\",sans-serif'>Homocysteine levels are\r\nstrongly influenced by diet, as well as by genetic factors.The dietary\r\ncomponents<\/span><\/p>\r\n\r\n<p class=MsoNormal style='text-autospace:none'><span lang=EN-US\r\nstyle='font-size:9.0pt;font-family:\"Arial\",sans-serif'>with the greatest\r\neffects are folic acid and vitamins B6 and B12.Folic acid and other B vitamins\r\nhelp break<\/span><\/p>\r\n\r\n<p class=MsoNormal style='text-autospace:none'><span lang=EN-US\r\nstyle='font-size:9.0pt;font-family:\"Arial\",sans-serif'>down homocysteine in the\r\nbody.Several studies have found that higher blood levels of B vitamins are\r\nrelated,<\/span><\/p>\r\n\r\n<p class=MsoNormal style='text-autospace:none'><span lang=EN-US\r\nstyle='font-size:9.0pt;font-family:\"Arial\",sans-serif'>at least partly, to\r\nlower concentrations of homocysteine.Other recent evidence shows that low blood\r\nlevels of<\/span><\/p>\r\n\r\n<p class=MsoNormal style='text-autospace:none'><span lang=EN-US\r\nstyle='font-size:9.0pt;font-family:\"Arial\",sans-serif'>folic acid are linked\r\nwith a higher risk of fatal coronary heart disease and stroke.<\/span><\/p>\r\n\r\n<p class=MsoNormal style='text-autospace:none'><span lang=EN-US\r\nstyle='font-size:9.0pt;font-family:\"Arial\",sans-serif'>Several clinical trials\r\nare under way to test whether lowering homocysteine will reduce CHD risk.Recent\r\ndata<\/span><\/p>\r\n\r\n<p class=MsoNormal style='text-autospace:none'><span lang=EN-US\r\nstyle='font-size:9.0pt;font-family:\"Arial\",sans-serif'>show that the\r\ninstitution of folate fortification of foods has reduced the average level of\r\nhomocysteine.Recent<\/span><\/p>\r\n\r\n<p class=MsoNormal style='text-autospace:none'><span lang=EN-US\r\nstyle='font-size:9.0pt;font-family:\"Arial\",sans-serif'>findings suggest that\r\nlaboratory testing for homocysteine levels can improve the assessment of risk.\r\nIt may be<\/span><\/p>\r\n\r\n<p class=MsoNormal style='text-autospace:none'><span lang=EN-US\r\nstyle='font-size:9.0pt;font-family:\"Arial\",sans-serif'>particularly useful in\r\npatients with a personal or family history of cardiovascular disease,but in\r\nwhom the<\/span><\/p>\r\n\r\n<p class=MsoNormal style='text-autospace:none'><span lang=EN-US\r\nstyle='font-size:9.0pt;font-family:\"Arial\",sans-serif'>well-established risk\r\nfactors (smoking,high blood cholesterol,high blood pressure) do not exist.\r\nAlthough evidence<\/span><\/p>\r\n\r\n<p class=MsoNormal style='text-autospace:none'><span lang=EN-US\r\nstyle='font-size:9.0pt;font-family:\"Arial\",sans-serif'>for the benefit of\r\nlowering homocysteine levels is lacking, patients at high risk should be\r\nstrongly advised to be<\/span><\/p>\r\n\r\n<p class=MsoNormal style='text-autospace:none'><span lang=EN-US\r\nstyle='font-size:9.0pt;font-family:\"Arial\",sans-serif'>sure to get enough folic\r\nacid and vitamins B6 and B12 in their diet. Foods high in folic acid include\r\ngreen,leafy<\/span><\/p>\r\n\r\n<p class=MsoNormal style='text-autospace:none'><span lang=EN-US\r\nstyle='font-size:9.0pt;font-family:\"Arial\",sans-serif'>vegetables and grain\r\nproducts fortified with folic acid.But this is just one risk factor. A\r\nphysician taking any type of<\/span><\/p>\r\n\r\n<p class=MsoNormal style='text-autospace:none'><span lang=EN-US\r\nstyle='font-size:9.0pt;font-family:\"Arial\",sans-serif'>nutritional approach to\r\nreducing risk should consider a person's overall risk factor profile and total\r\ndiet.<\/span><\/p>\r\n\r\n<p class=MsoNormal style='text-autospace:none'><span lang=EN-US\r\nstyle='font-size:9.0pt;font-family:\"Arial\",sans-serif'>The test is not affected\r\nby Bilirubin up to 20mg\/dL, Hemoglobin up to 1000mg\/dL, triglycerides up to\r\n1000mg\/dL.<\/span><\/p>\r\n\r\n<p class=MsoNormal style='text-autospace:none'><span lang=EN-US\r\nstyle='font-size:9.0pt;font-family:\"Arial\",sans-serif'>All specimens must be\r\nplaced on ice immediately after collection and should be centrifuged and\r\nseparated from<\/span><\/p>\r\n\r\n<p class=MsoNormal style='text-autospace:none'><span lang=EN-US\r\nstyle='font-size:9.0pt;font-family:\"Arial\",sans-serif'>cells as soon as\r\npossible (within one hour).Specimens should be stored tightly capped at 2-8\r\ndegree C for upto<\/span><\/p>\r\n\r\n<p class=MsoNormal><span lang=EN-US style='font-size:9.0pt;font-family:\"Arial\",sans-serif'>48\r\nhours, or frozen at -20 degree C if testing is delayed further.<\/span><\/p>\r\n\r\n<p class=MsoNormal><span lang=EN-US style='font-size:11.0pt;font-family:\"Bookman Old Style\",serif'>&nbsp;<\/span><\/p>\r\n\r\n<p class=MsoNormal><span lang=EN-US style='font-size:11.0pt;font-family:\"Bookman Old Style\",serif'>&nbsp;<\/span><\/p>\r\n\r\n<p class=MsoNormal><span lang=EN-US style='font-size:11.0pt;font-family:\"Bookman Old Style\",serif'>&nbsp;<\/span><\/p>\r\n\r\n<p class=MsoNormal><span lang=EN-US>&nbsp;<\/span><\/p>\r\n\r\n<\/div>","page":{"size":"612.0pt 792.0pt","margin":"148.5pt 49.5pt 72.0pt 31.5pt"}}